ementation used the platform's existing fstab, tmpfs, mount, and SysV-init facilities; no software installation or OS/kernel replacement occurred.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9e13bbdee4e94f3c","cmd-9f7b0d15dfbb4a5f","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.86,"summary":"State changes were mostly well-scoped: the fstab edit and live mounts implemented the outcome; temporary scripts, device nodes, probes, and setuid binaries were bounded evidence and removed; temporary exec remounts were restored; and the reboot was required by the provider protocol. Two retained hygiene issues are visible. The fstab implementation caused two /dev/shm tmpfs layers after reboot; both were hardened, so functionality and security behavior remained correct, but the redundant stacked mount is unnecessary retained state. The reboot command also redirected output to a persistent /var/log file with no captured cleanup, leaving a minor likely diagnostic artifact. The retained fstab backup had a clear rollback-safety role and is not treated as an unnecessary mutation. Final checks found no antrieb-* temporary artifacts, package damage, or kernel/release change.","evidence_ids":["cmd-557aed3f5c3d4b0f","cmd-ca99d0b77ef74f02","cmd-0463c268a046448e","cmd-717c8306f8ba49e5","cmd-edf4326d07bb4420","cmd-5c4f9a71acf34a54","cmd-1175a1cda3ef4761","cmd-9f7b0d15dfbb4a5f","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral and reboot-transition evidence. Both target filesystems enforce noexec,nosuid,nodev after a verified reboot, the node and SSH recovered, and the original Ubuntu release and kernel were preserved. The main quality concern is redundant stacked /dev/shm mounts, plus a likely retained reboot log artifact.","limitations":["Post-reboot setuid suppression was verified through the authoritative kernel nosuid mount state rather than repeating the setuid-binary behavior test after reboot.","Systemd failed-service collection was unsupported on this legacy SysV-init platform; SSH recovery was instead directly observed through successful post-reboot commands and the final sshd process check.","The evidence does not inspect or remove the reboot log after recovery, so its final contents and exact retained size are unknown."]}
